# Assignment J-OM8 — synchronous exact expert fill via parallel buffered pread (component; host jetson-arm; worker Codex)
Repo root: /home/muk/projects/siminfeng/campaign. Engine tree: ref/llama.cpp-up1108 @ cf34d311 (do NOT modify it; work in a copy, e.g. `git -C ref/llama.cpp-up1108 worktree add /tmp/j-om8-src cf34d311` or cp -a, then apply patches there).
Read first: cc/mmap_advice/README.md, cc/mmap_advice/patches/02-router-prefetch.patch (gives you a CPU-scheduler observer hook that sees the router top-k ids on host before the CPU expert matmul: `llama_context::expert_prefetch_cb`, `ggml_backend_sched_set_cpu_callback`), decisions/D033-readahead-tradeoff-iops-bound.md, CLOSEOUT.md section 2.4 rows ra-/om7- and C2/C3, experiments/jetson/OM/qwen36-layout.json.

## Why
Over-memory decode (Qwen3.6-35B-A3B, 20.8 GiB on 7.4 GiB, `-ngl 99 -cmoe -lm mmap`) is NVMe-bound: ~56 GiB read per 64-token run at ~95 KiB/IO, 57% of bytes unused (default readahead). J-OM7 issued MADV_WILLNEED at the hook: zero lead time, so faults still read 4-5 KiB pages and decode fell 38%. The drive does 536 MiB/s at 576 KiB qd8 but 20 MiB/s at 4 KiB qd1. Hypothesis: make the needed expert bytes resident *synchronously and with large parallel reads* right at the hook, so the CPU matmul only takes minor faults.

## Deliverables
1. `cc/expert_fill/expert_fill.h` (header-only C++17, Linux): a `ExpertFiller` with a persistent thread pool (N threads, env `LLAMA_EXPERT_FILL_THREADS`, default 8), its own `O_RDONLY` fd on the model file (no O_DIRECT; buffered pread so the page cache, which backs the mmap, is filled), and `fill(const std::vector<Range>&)` that: page-aligns each (file offset, len) outward, merges overlapping/adjacent ranges, uses `mincore()` on the corresponding mmap address range to skip already-resident pages (only non-resident runs are read), splits runs into chunks of `LLAMA_EXPERT_FILL_CHUNK_KIB` (default 1024) and preads them in parallel into per-thread scratch buffers, and returns only when all reads completed. Counters: calls, ranges, bytes requested, bytes skipped resident, bytes read, read syscalls, total fill wall ns, max single-fill ns.
2. `cc/expert_fill/test_expert_fill.cpp` + `cc/expert_fill/Makefile` (`make test` builds with g++ -O2 -pthread and runs): creates a 64 MiB temp file, mmaps it, evicts with `posix_fadvise(POSIX_FADV_DONTNEED)` (no root needed), fills 3 overlapping ranges, asserts via mincore that exactly the page-aligned union is resident (allow kernel readahead to make *extra* pages resident only if you measured it; assert at least the union), asserts counters (bytes_read <= union size rounded to pages, second identical fill reads 0 bytes), and asserts data read through the mmap equals file contents. Must pass on this host.
3. `cc/mmap_advice/patches/03-expert-fill.patch` (applies on top of 01+02) and `cc/mmap_advice/patches/combined-01-02-03.patch`: opt-in env `LLAMA_EXPERT_FILL=1`. At the same hook as 02, compute the deduped selected expert ids of layer L for the ubatch, map them to the gate/up/down slices (file offset = tensor file offset + id*nb[2]; you must obtain *file* offsets, not just mmap addresses — find where llama_model_loader keeps per-tensor offsets/files and expose what you need), and call `ExpertFiller::fill` synchronously before returning from the callback. The patch adds `src/llama-expert-fill.h` with the same content as cc/expert_fill/expert_fill.h (state this; one source of truth = cc/expert_fill/expert_fill.h, copied into the patch). `LLAMA_EXPERT_FILL=1` must NOT enable the WILLNEED prefetch of 02; MADV_RANDOM on expert ranges is controlled separately by `LLAMA_EXPERT_FILL_RANDOM=1` (default off) so both variants can be A/B'd. Startup log line exactly starting `expert_fill: active` with threads/chunk/random settings; exit log line `expert_fill: stats` with all counters. If tensors are not mmap-backed or file offsets unavailable: log `expert_fill: inactive (<reason>)` once and do nothing.
4. Syntax/compile proof without a full build: apply combined-01-02-03 to your copy and compile the touched translation units with `g++ -std=c++17 -fsyntax-only` using the tree's include dirs (`-Iinclude -Iggml/include -Isrc -Iggml/src` etc.; find the right set). Record the exact command and output in `cc/expert_fill/COMPILE-CHECK.txt`.
5. `cc/expert_fill/README.md` (<= 60 lines): mechanism, expected effect with arithmetic (bytes/token at LRU-like page-cache hit rates from CLOSEOUT 3.D; drive ceiling 536-573 MiB/s), risks (page-cache thrash: the fill can evict experts needed later in the same token; CPU time in memcpy for resident pages avoided by mincore), and the A/B spec to run: arms base / fill / fill+random, ABCCBA, llama-completion p1 `-n 64 -c 4096 --temp 0 -s 1 -no-cnv -ngl 99 -lm mmap -cmoe`, switch regex `expert_fill: active`, metrics eval_tps, nvme_gib, avg_kib_per_io. Also write that spec as `cc/abrun/specs/J-OM8.json` using the schema in assignments/H2-abrun.md section 4 (image name `campaign/llamacpp:jetson-up1108-om8`).

